<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mustafa Chokay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mustafa Shokay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mustafa Chokay was a Kazakh lawyer and politician who headed the Turkestan Autonomy, proclaimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> in 1917 within a hoped-for democratic Russian federation. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> destroyed Kokand in February 1918 he escaped, eventually settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he edited émigré journals and campaigned for Turkestani independence until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-chokay.jpg" alt="Mustafa Chokay" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D. V. Nazarov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mustafa_Shokay_in_youth_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mustafa Shokay was born on 25 December 1890 into a wealthy Kazakh family of the Middle Juz at Ak-Meshit, the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyzylorda">Kyzylorda</a>. His grandfather had served as datkha, a chief minister, to the khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>, and his mother Bakty, who knew Kazakh, Arabic and Persian, taught her children to read and write. He went to a Russian school, entered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> gymnasium in 1902 and graduated in 1910 with its gold medal, which General Alexander Samsonov tried to give to a Russian student instead. He then studied law at the university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a> from 1910 to 1917, interrupted when his father died in 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Recommended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alikhan-bukeikhanov">Alikhan Bukeikhanov</a>, he worked as secretary and translator of the Muslim faction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a>, where he befriended Ahmad Zeki Velidi and helped prepare the protests against the decree of 25 June 1916 drafting Central Asians for labour behind the front, which set off a great uprising. In 1917 he was a delegate to the Muslim congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, sat on the Provisional Government's Turkestan committee and was elected chairman of Turkestan's national committee. When Bolshevik troops sent on 11 February 1918 destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>, he fled through Fergana to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> with a price of 1000 roubles on his head, and in April 1918 he married Maria Gorina.</p><p>By way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> the couple reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, where they lived until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took the city in February 1921, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. With the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kerensky">Alexander Kerensky</a> they moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in the summer of 1921, and in 1923 to Nogent-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>. Shokay wrote for émigré papers, founded Yash Turkistan in Istanbul in 1927 and edited the journal Yash Turkestan, which ran to 117 issues before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. His books included Chez les Soviets en Asie Centrale of 1928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 22 June 1941 the German occupiers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> interned him at Compiègne and then took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he was asked to lead the Turkestan Legion of Turkic Soviet prisoners. Accounts of his life say he was shocked by the prisoners' conditions and wrote to Joachim von Ribbentrop refusing further cooperation. He died in a Berlin hospital on 27 December 1941, officially of blood poisoning after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>, though his wife held that he had been immune and poisoning has been suspected. A memorial stele stands in Nogent-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>, and streets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyzylorda">Kyzylorda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a> bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mustafa Shokay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government, led first by Muhammadjan Tynyshbaev and then by <b>Mustafa Chokay</b>, included Jadid reformers and some Russian members.</blockquote><a href="/state/kokand-autonomy">Kokand Autonomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyzylorda">Kyzylorda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.85,65.5&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Kyzylorda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6111 579 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mustafa Chokay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6561 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6561 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.85,65.5&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
